00:00:14.982 --> 00:00:16.820
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: So
welcome, everyone.

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:20.870
Welcome to GCP NEXT 2016.

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:23.380
I'm Prajakta, product manager
in the Cloud Networking

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:27.890
team at Google in the
Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:30.380
So let's start with you today.

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:33.590
You just sold your CIO on the
idea of moving your workloads

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:34.880
to the Google Cloud.

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:36.200
Congratulations.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:38.190
Yeah, you started first
with saying, yeah,

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:40.950
we have on-demand
access to compute.

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:43.162
You're going to
save tons of money.

00:00:43.162 --> 00:00:47.020
But what really clinched
the deal for you was this--

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:51.130
Google Cloud Platform's
world-class infrastructure

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:55.705
and Google Cloud Platform's
best-of-the-breed services.

00:00:55.705 --> 00:00:59.080
That is why Google Cloud
Platform gives you the ability

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:01.850
to deliver your
services anywhere

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:03.430
anytime across the globe.

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:09.130
So how does Google Cloud
Platform deliver your services

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:12.240
reliably across the globe
with high performance

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.290
and with low latency?

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:18.260
A key cloud service that lets
it do this is Google Global Load

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:19.770
Balancing.

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:22.930
And that is the focus
of my talk today.

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:25.510
I'm sure all of you are
familiar with load balancers,

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:30.316
but maybe let's do
a Load Balancer 101.

00:01:30.316 --> 00:01:31.690
Now, load balance
is what created

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:35.080
to deliver high availability
and to scale the services.

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:37.860
And if you look at a load
balancer, as you can see there,

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:39.340
you have your back ends.

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:42.150
These are the ones that
actually service your clients.

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:44.200
You stick a load
balancer in front,

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:47.420
you put a front-end VIP
on it, and voila, you

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:50.140
have a service where
the load is distributed

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:52.370
across all of these servers.

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:53.870
You have a service
that's resilient,

00:01:53.870 --> 00:01:56.900
because if one of these
fails, your load balancer will

00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:00.780
seamlessly route the request
to another healthy instance.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.010
You can scale your service
now, because you simply

00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:05.740
stick another server
there and it scales up.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:07.380
And lastly, a load
balancer is the one

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:08.630
that hosts your front-end VIP.

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:14.560
So one more thing that I talked
about in the previous one--

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:17.092
it's Load Balancing 101.

00:02:17.092 --> 00:02:18.550
Now let's look at
it from the point

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:21.070
of view of a cloud provider.

00:02:21.070 --> 00:02:22.300
Look at the cloud.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:24.380
You have a multi-tenant
environment.

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:26.400
You have thousands of users.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:29.400
You have compute resources that
are scattered across the world.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:32.880
And then you need the
ability to seamlessly scale

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:36.730
as the number of your users
and the traffic grows.

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:37.900
So you look here.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:40.450
You see three or four
flavors of load balancing.

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:43.200
The first one is your
hardware load balancers.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:44.520
Think of them.

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.430
I'm sure all of
you have used them.

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:48.230
They have fixed form factor.

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:50.760
So if you need more
throughput, what do you do?

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:53.110
You go and buy a bigger box.

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:55.512
If you need more
features, what do you do?

00:02:55.512 --> 00:02:57.720
You approach the vendor,
and you wait for the release

00:02:57.720 --> 00:02:59.600
to put the features in.

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:02.570
HA is pretty much active-active
or active-standby.

00:03:02.570 --> 00:03:04.720
And these are definitely
expensive to load-balance

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:08.510
at scale, especially
cloud scale.

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:10.550
Next come the software
load balancers.

00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:12.410
And when I talk of
software load balancers,

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:15.990
I mean load balancing logic
that runs in a VM instance.

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:18.090
Could be a cluster.

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:19.110
Right?

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:21.250
Now, these are
definitely a step up,

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:24.900
but is that enough to deliver
cloud-scale load balancing?

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:27.290
That is where the Google
Cloud Platform Global Load

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:28.990
Balancing comes in.

00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:31.950
It's basically a
software-defined, globally

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:34.190
distributed load balancing.

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:36.930
That is exactly what Google
Global Load Balancing is.

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:38.874
And today I wanted to
shed some light on it.

00:03:38.874 --> 00:03:40.790
So there are some
interesting aspects about it

00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:43.440
that I wanted to highlight.

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:45.440
So first, let's
start with you again.

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:48.430
Why do you want global
application delivery?

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:51.610
Maybe you want to deliver your
services around the globe.

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:54.120
Maybe you need the highest
levels of availability,

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:56.780
so you want to replicate your
services around the globe

00:03:56.780 --> 00:04:00.240
or you want to deliver your
services with low latency

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:03.030
close to wherever
your users are.

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:06.790
That is what Google Global Load
Balancing enables you to do.

00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:10.510
Instantiate your workloads
anywhere you want in the globe.

00:04:10.510 --> 00:04:14.050
But unlike traditional,
DNS-based load balancing

00:04:14.050 --> 00:04:17.339
solutions, you don't have
to front-end each region

00:04:17.339 --> 00:04:19.560
with its own IP.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:22.430
You can front-end
them with a single IP.

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:26.210
Finally, you can deliver low
latency and high bandwidth

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:29.120
connectivity for all your users.

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:32.480
And with all of this, you can
think of Google Global Load

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:35.480
Balancing as having
infinite capacity.

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:37.950
So as your users and
your traffic increase,

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:39.060
it just seamlessly scales.

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:45.610
Now let's take maybe a
sneak peek at what's inside.

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:48.930
Because often, I get asked,
where is the Google Global Load

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:50.240
Balancer?

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:52.280
The answer is it's everywhere.

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:55.400
It's not a single box,
it's not running in a VM,

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:57.340
it's not a cluster.

00:04:57.340 --> 00:04:59.770
It's load balancing
distributed across the world.

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:01.630
And there are four
key technologies

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:05.660
that make this happen--
one, software-defined load

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:08.960
balancing; two,
Google's global network;

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:15.990
three, seamless autoscaling; and
fourth is intelligent anycast.

00:05:15.990 --> 00:05:17.970
So how many of
you have not heard

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:19.290
of software-defined networking?

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:21.390
Raise your hand.

00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:22.170
OK.

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:23.450
That's great.

00:05:23.450 --> 00:05:25.900
So basically, this is
software-defined load

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:28.020
balancing, which
builds on the concepts

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:30.660
of software-defined networking.

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:32.010
Now let's look at the right.

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:35.530
As you can see there, that is
what the Google Global Load

00:05:35.530 --> 00:05:37.140
Balancer does.

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:39.380
If you look at the
traditional load balancer,

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:41.550
you see a single load balancer.

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:44.620
If you look on the right, which
is the Google side of things,

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:46.640
there is no load balancer.

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:49.030
And the reason for
that is load balancing

00:05:49.030 --> 00:05:51.650
is done by distributed,
software-defined entities

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:53.410
called Google front ends.

00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:56.120
We call them GFEs very lovingly.

00:05:56.120 --> 00:06:00.340
And the GFEs themselves are
distributed across the world.

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:02.670
And they work in
conjunction with a number

00:06:02.670 --> 00:06:04.700
of other
software-defined systems

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:07.277
to do global load balancing.

00:06:07.277 --> 00:06:09.610
Now, what they really enable
you to do-- as you can see,

00:06:09.610 --> 00:06:11.401
they are at the edge
of the Google network.

00:06:11.401 --> 00:06:13.480
And you can see
a number of GFEs.

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:16.020
So when somebody says,
where is the load balancing,

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:17.580
it's in each of
these GFEs, which

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:19.960
are scattered around the word.

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:23.090
The GFEs enable you to terminate
your user traffic closest

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:24.660
to your user.

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:26.320
And they load-balance
the user request

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:29.940
to the closest healthy back
end with available capacity.

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:32.500
So they do work with other
software-defined systems

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:34.490
to do very sophisticated
load balancing, which

00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:37.067
is a function of
proximity and capacity--

00:06:37.067 --> 00:06:38.525
so available
capacity, essentially.

00:06:41.770 --> 00:06:44.410
Now let's look at the
second key enabler.

00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:47.170
That's Google's global network.

00:06:47.170 --> 00:06:51.090
Google has 70+ edge POPs
across 33 countries.

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:53.930
And it has a high-quality
private network

00:06:53.930 --> 00:06:56.240
for edge-to-data
center connectivity.

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:58.887
So each of these are
connected to the data center,

00:06:58.887 --> 00:07:01.470
and you are computing the data
centers through this very, very

00:07:01.470 --> 00:07:03.620
high-quality network.

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:07.830
Now, out of these 70 edge
POPs, these load-balancing GFEs

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:12.040
are placed in 50 of these
POPs close to your users

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:13.790
across the globe.

00:07:13.790 --> 00:07:17.740
So again, as I said before,
if anybody asks you,

00:07:17.740 --> 00:07:20.600
where is the GCP
Global Load Balancer,

00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:24.180
the answer is it's at
all of these 50-plus POPs

00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:27.030
across the globe.

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:31.680
Now let's look at these two
diagrams a little bit closely.

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:33.480
Because your next
question should be,

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:35.850
sure, you let me put this
close to the user, what's

00:07:35.850 --> 00:07:37.620
the benefit for me?

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:39.360
Look at the left.

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:42.480
That's your traditional
cloud provider.

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:44.440
Your user's request
is going to traverse

00:07:44.440 --> 00:07:47.370
just the general internet
for a majority of time

00:07:47.370 --> 00:07:49.810
and finally reach the load
balancer in your cloud

00:07:49.810 --> 00:07:52.760
provider's data center.

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:56.250
Now let's look at the Google
Global Cloud Load Balancing.

00:07:56.250 --> 00:07:58.170
If you notice, the
GFEs are sitting

00:07:58.170 --> 00:08:00.130
very close to your user.

00:08:00.130 --> 00:08:03.620
So the first line, which
is your TCP session one,

00:08:03.620 --> 00:08:07.490
your user request is barely
traversing the internet, right?

00:08:07.490 --> 00:08:09.110
And this means
because it traverses

00:08:09.110 --> 00:08:11.730
the internet for a
short time, beyond that,

00:08:11.730 --> 00:08:13.570
the GFEs will make
sure that they

00:08:13.570 --> 00:08:16.170
deliver your request
over Google's

00:08:16.170 --> 00:08:18.300
high-quality private network.

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:20.320
And what does this do for you?

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:24.370
The benefits are significant--
reduced latency and improved

00:08:24.370 --> 00:08:26.450
throughput for your users.

00:08:26.450 --> 00:08:29.310
Delivering unflappable
services even in the wake

00:08:29.310 --> 00:08:32.679
of stability or instability.

00:08:32.679 --> 00:08:34.140
And one thing which
is not obvious

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:36.280
but which I wanted
to point out--

00:08:36.280 --> 00:08:39.780
serving static, cachable
contents from multiple points

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:42.559
near your users.

00:08:42.559 --> 00:08:45.185
So why doesn't
everybody do this?

00:08:45.185 --> 00:08:48.540
This way of load balancing
is not easy to architect.

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:52.040
It needs a global network, and
it needs sophisticated software

00:08:52.040 --> 00:08:54.040
and distributed technologies.

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:56.425
And Google has been doing
this for the last 15 years.

00:08:56.425 --> 00:08:58.800
And that's what we are really
leveraging for Google Cloud

00:08:58.800 --> 00:08:59.425
Load Balancing.

00:09:02.550 --> 00:09:04.642
So let's come to the third part.

00:09:04.642 --> 00:09:07.810
A question we often
get asked-- how many

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:10.980
VMs should I provision
for my service?

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:12.840
For that, the first
thing you should do

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:15.080
is look at your workload.

00:09:15.080 --> 00:09:17.210
Maybe your business
is growing fast,

00:09:17.210 --> 00:09:19.920
so you need to
scale up steadily.

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:22.570
Or maybe you have
bursty traffic.

00:09:22.570 --> 00:09:25.300
Or maybe your traffic
is on and off in nature.

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:27.830
Maybe you're running
batch workloads.

00:09:27.830 --> 00:09:31.500
Then you come to the classic
resource allocation problem.

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:35.100
If you provision for peak,
you're wasting resources.

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:39.350
If you provision for average,
you are in big trouble.

00:09:39.350 --> 00:09:43.020
So the ideal way is to provision
in line with your traffic.

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:46.810
And this is where Google Cloud
Platform Autoscaling comes in.

00:09:46.810 --> 00:09:50.010
It intelligently adjusts
resources based on load.

00:09:50.010 --> 00:09:53.780
So if your traffic grows,
so do your resources.

00:09:53.780 --> 00:09:58.270
If your traffic shrinks, your
resources shrink as well.

00:09:58.270 --> 00:10:01.250
But most importantly,
to do this well,

00:10:01.250 --> 00:10:03.160
this autoscaling
does the scale-up

00:10:03.160 --> 00:10:04.860
in a matter of seconds.

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:06.692
There is no pre-warming needed.

00:10:06.692 --> 00:10:08.650
So if there was one
takeaway about autoscaling,

00:10:08.650 --> 00:10:11.810
I want you to take
that away with you.

00:10:11.810 --> 00:10:14.470
Now, how do you
provision autoscaling?

00:10:14.470 --> 00:10:17.420
So basically, you provision
autoscale instance groups

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:19.660
and you specify an
autoscaling policy.

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:22.160
It's a very intuitive,
intent-based policy.

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:27.010
You say stuff like, OK, keep
my VMs at 85% utilization.

00:10:27.010 --> 00:10:30.510
Now, Autoscaler, it'll
monitor your instances,

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:33.460
it'll work closely with
the Global Load Balancer,

00:10:33.460 --> 00:10:36.800
and it makes sure that if
the capacity of your VMs

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:39.080
goes below the
configured threshold,

00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:41.180
it'll scale them up in
a matter of seconds.

00:10:44.150 --> 00:10:46.590
The last key technology
I wanted to talk about

00:10:46.590 --> 00:10:48.360
is Intelligent Anycast.

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:50.080
Now, let's look
at this technology

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:53.030
through the lens of
an actual deployment.

00:10:53.030 --> 00:10:56.450
Let's say you decided
to host www.myapp.com

00:10:56.450 --> 00:10:57.680
in the Google Cloud.

00:10:57.680 --> 00:11:00.130
Your users are all
over the world,

00:11:00.130 --> 00:11:03.910
so you put your compute
instances in three regions.

00:11:03.910 --> 00:11:06.770
Let's say you put them in US
central Google data centers

00:11:06.770 --> 00:11:10.350
in Europe and then in Asia.

00:11:10.350 --> 00:11:12.900
Now, normally with any
other cloud provider,

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:16.240
you would have had three
DNS VIPs, one per region.

00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:18.400
And then you would have
to use DNS load balancing

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:21.660
to sort of send them
to the correct region.

00:11:21.660 --> 00:11:23.920
If you have used
DNS-based load balancers--

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:26.299
so like global server
load balancers--

00:11:26.299 --> 00:11:27.840
I can say that
confidently, because I

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:29.630
don't code for a lot of them.

00:11:29.630 --> 00:11:33.020
You have a bunch of issues
that you have to deal with.

00:11:33.020 --> 00:11:35.000
First of all, DNS-based
load balancers

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:39.540
take minutes if not hours to
detect and react to change.

00:11:39.540 --> 00:11:41.600
They also suffer
from resolver caching

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:44.792
and other well-known
detail-based issues.

00:11:44.792 --> 00:11:46.920
Now, if you look
at it in contrast,

00:11:46.920 --> 00:11:49.220
let's look at the
Intelligent Anycast.

00:11:49.220 --> 00:11:52.070
You have a single IP, so there
is no problem with caching.

00:11:52.070 --> 00:11:55.470
Wherever you go,
it's the same IP.

00:11:55.470 --> 00:11:59.950
What that means is Maya in
California, Bob in London,

00:11:59.950 --> 00:12:04.110
and then Shen in Singapore,
they each use 120.1.1.1

00:12:04.110 --> 00:12:05.490
to reach your
service, myapp.com.

00:12:08.130 --> 00:12:11.160
Intelligent Anycast
also directs them

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:13.110
to the resource
that's closest to them

00:12:13.110 --> 00:12:15.160
with available capacity.

00:12:15.160 --> 00:12:17.740
And there are a lot of things
that I have not put here.

00:12:17.740 --> 00:12:19.740
But for instance, if
they are routing flaps,

00:12:19.740 --> 00:12:21.310
it'll ensure that
your request goes

00:12:21.310 --> 00:12:25.780
to the correct place, the place
that can serve it reliability

00:12:25.780 --> 00:12:29.450
and can serve it well.

00:12:29.450 --> 00:12:32.680
So your next question will
be, yes, this sounds great,

00:12:32.680 --> 00:12:35.300
how do I get started?

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:37.140
Just go to the Google
Cloud Platform website

00:12:37.140 --> 00:12:38.940
and get started configuring it.

00:12:38.940 --> 00:12:41.450
Select the flavor of your load
balancers-- whether you want

00:12:41.450 --> 00:12:43.990
HTTP as load balancing
in this case.

00:12:43.990 --> 00:12:46.220
Select and deploy
your back ends.

00:12:46.220 --> 00:12:50.789
Consider a front-end Anycast
VIP, and you're done.

00:12:50.789 --> 00:12:52.580
I don't know if you
can see things clearly,

00:12:52.580 --> 00:12:54.600
but what I wanted to
show is a new UI that's

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:56.460
making its debut next month.

00:12:56.460 --> 00:12:58.820
It's as simple as just picking
the first option, start

00:12:58.820 --> 00:12:59.860
configuring.

00:12:59.860 --> 00:13:02.591
And then you go considering the
three steps that we put there.

00:13:02.591 --> 00:13:04.340
I purposely didn't
show you the other two,

00:13:04.340 --> 00:13:06.510
because I want to highly
encourage you to go and try

00:13:06.510 --> 00:13:09.470
them out yourself.

00:13:09.470 --> 00:13:13.840
So honestly, the biggest
benefit or the biggest advantage

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:18.260
that Google Cloud Load Balancing
delivers is peace of mind.

00:13:18.260 --> 00:13:20.340
You can see the quote
on the screen, right?

00:13:20.340 --> 00:13:22.720
That is one happy
customer on the screen.

00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:24.910
And there are many more
who would say the same.

00:13:24.910 --> 00:13:26.800
So I highly encourage
you to try it,

00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:31.060
and I'm sure it will give you
the same peace of mind as well.

00:13:31.060 --> 00:13:33.630
One last interesting thing
I wanted to point out

00:13:33.630 --> 00:13:36.730
is that Google Global Load
Balancing not only delivers

00:13:36.730 --> 00:13:38.950
your cloud services,
but it's just

00:13:38.950 --> 00:13:41.940
as essential to
Google's own services.

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:45.360
It uses the same set of
software-defined technologies

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:47.450
and the same global
infrastructure

00:13:47.450 --> 00:13:50.410
that are being used to
deliver Google Search, Gmail,

00:13:50.410 --> 00:13:52.560
and other Google services.

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:54.110
So definitely use
this information

00:13:54.110 --> 00:13:57.310
when you're trying to clinch
getting your CIO to move

00:13:57.310 --> 00:13:59.100
the workloads to the cloud.

00:13:59.100 --> 00:14:01.330
So with that, it's
time to see Global Load

00:14:01.330 --> 00:14:03.350
Balancing in action.

00:14:03.350 --> 00:14:06.260
Please welcome our next speaker,
Anna, tech lead for Global Load

00:14:06.260 --> 00:14:10.350
Balancing, to show you how to
deliver reliable services not

00:14:10.350 --> 00:14:12.800
just at cloud scale,
but at not full scale.

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:13.300
Thank you.

00:14:13.300 --> 00:14:14.230
Hey, Anna.

00:14:14.230 --> 00:14:17.583
[APPLAUSE]

00:14:20.460 --> 00:14:23.470
ANNA BERENBERG:
Thank you, Prajakta.

00:14:23.470 --> 00:14:29.260
Today we will be looking beyond
cloud scale availability.

00:14:29.260 --> 00:14:32.560
All cloud providers
publish their SLA.

00:14:32.560 --> 00:14:35.990
Some publish 3 nines, some
publish 3 and 1/2 nines

00:14:35.990 --> 00:14:37.290
like GCP.

00:14:37.290 --> 00:14:40.730
But what if you want to
build an application that

00:14:40.730 --> 00:14:43.630
has 5 nine availabilities
or even beyond that?

00:14:43.630 --> 00:14:47.660
That's how Google
built its applications.

00:14:47.660 --> 00:14:49.660
There is a short
answer for that.

00:14:49.660 --> 00:14:51.720
Use Global Load Balancing.

00:14:51.720 --> 00:14:58.230
With Global Load Balancing, you
get worldwide capacity anytime

00:14:58.230 --> 00:15:00.130
anything happens.

00:15:00.130 --> 00:15:05.020
You get the protection
against region-wide failures.

00:15:05.020 --> 00:15:10.620
And with cross-regional overflow
and cross-regional failover,

00:15:10.620 --> 00:15:15.220
you're getting highly
available solution.

00:15:15.220 --> 00:15:16.990
Today they're
going to be working

00:15:16.990 --> 00:15:21.760
through the example of highly
available Globally Load

00:15:21.760 --> 00:15:25.960
Balanced service, Santa Tracker.

00:15:25.960 --> 00:15:30.620
Who doesn't like Santa, whether
you're a child, an adult, or in

00:15:30.620 --> 00:15:31.700
between.

00:15:31.700 --> 00:15:36.000
It's always fun to watch
Santa to deliver presents.

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:40.440
And while Santa is busy
delivering presents

00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:44.700
around the world, you
and I, as service owners,

00:15:44.700 --> 00:15:48.310
will pay attention to
traffic volume and shape,

00:15:48.310 --> 00:15:52.720
and get to experience a series
of disasters that typically

00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:54.660
happen over service lifetime.

00:15:57.930 --> 00:16:02.970
We'll start with configuring
Santa Tracker Service on GCP

00:16:02.970 --> 00:16:06.241
with configuring it
in three continents.

00:16:06.241 --> 00:16:06.740
Why?

00:16:06.740 --> 00:16:08.870
Because service is
global, and Santa's

00:16:08.870 --> 00:16:11.130
going to be traveling
around the world.

00:16:11.130 --> 00:16:15.410
We will create one instance
group in Asia, one in Europe,

00:16:15.410 --> 00:16:18.070
and two in United
States to account

00:16:18.070 --> 00:16:20.470
for anticipated traffic.

00:16:20.470 --> 00:16:24.930
Unfortunately, I was up all
night prepping for our talk.

00:16:24.930 --> 00:16:28.720
And after playing with the
manual added instances,

00:16:28.720 --> 00:16:32.650
I forgot to enable
Autoscaler in Asia.

00:16:32.650 --> 00:16:33.770
So watch for it.

00:16:33.770 --> 00:16:35.230
It's going to bite us later on.

00:16:38.090 --> 00:16:44.300
As the days start, Santa is hard
at work delivering presents.

00:16:44.300 --> 00:16:47.120
And while children and
adult watching him to drop

00:16:47.120 --> 00:16:51.300
presents through the chimneys,
you and I looking at a console.

00:16:51.300 --> 00:16:52.330
And guess what?

00:16:52.330 --> 00:16:56.350
The traffic volume is a little
higher than anticipated.

00:16:56.350 --> 00:16:58.400
In fact, it is a lot higher.

00:16:58.400 --> 00:17:02.540
So on one hand, we are happy
that our service is so popular.

00:17:02.540 --> 00:17:06.579
On another hand, we don't
know what's going to happen.

00:17:06.579 --> 00:17:08.745
Do we have enough
capacity to serve?

00:17:08.745 --> 00:17:12.030
Are we prepared for success?

00:17:12.030 --> 00:17:15.569
And indeed, interest in
Santa is not letting up.

00:17:15.569 --> 00:17:19.680
And now we have a lot
of traffic incoming.

00:17:19.680 --> 00:17:23.099
And suddenly, we see
overflow to Europe.

00:17:23.099 --> 00:17:25.440
What just happened?

00:17:25.440 --> 00:17:29.310
Well, what happened-- we go back
and look at our configuration

00:17:29.310 --> 00:17:32.950
and we figure out that we
didn't have Autoscaler enabled.

00:17:32.950 --> 00:17:36.790
So our Global Load
Balancer saved us

00:17:36.790 --> 00:17:41.290
from the outage and
overflow excess of traffic

00:17:41.290 --> 00:17:43.970
to closest region.

00:17:43.970 --> 00:17:46.790
But then without
any intervention.

00:17:46.790 --> 00:17:50.770
So we go back,
enable Autoscaler.

00:17:50.770 --> 00:17:56.480
And as Autoscaler is starting
instances, health check,

00:17:56.480 --> 00:17:59.020
and they are ready
to serve, the traffic

00:17:59.020 --> 00:18:04.250
is moving back to China,
to Asia, serving locally

00:18:04.250 --> 00:18:06.990
with a minimal latency.

00:18:06.990 --> 00:18:12.920
As day goes by, now
Santa is in Europe.

00:18:12.920 --> 00:18:18.270
Our instances in Europe just
got new binary with support

00:18:18.270 --> 00:18:22.930
for elvish language.

00:18:22.930 --> 00:18:27.200
Unfortunately, the feature
wasn't tested well.

00:18:27.200 --> 00:18:34.500
So the first time elves,
the Santa helpers,

00:18:34.500 --> 00:18:38.120
tried to check the
status of Santa

00:18:38.120 --> 00:18:41.960
Tracker, the binary crash.

00:18:41.960 --> 00:18:42.960
Don't panic.

00:18:42.960 --> 00:18:44.310
Nothing happens.

00:18:44.310 --> 00:18:46.950
Our Global Load
Balancer immediately

00:18:46.950 --> 00:18:50.690
recognized the health
of failed instances

00:18:50.690 --> 00:18:54.010
and moved traffic away.

00:18:54.010 --> 00:18:59.680
Let's stop here for a second
and analyze what just happened.

00:18:59.680 --> 00:19:03.220
There are no other
cloud provider that

00:19:03.220 --> 00:19:06.740
has cross-regional failovers.

00:19:06.740 --> 00:19:11.600
And let's see whether
regional failover would work.

00:19:11.600 --> 00:19:14.860
And it wouldn't, because all
binaries within the region

00:19:14.860 --> 00:19:16.840
are running the same version.

00:19:16.840 --> 00:19:21.080
So as long as elves continue
to send query of death,

00:19:21.080 --> 00:19:22.950
the binary would
continue to crash.

00:19:26.510 --> 00:19:29.170
Our Global Load Balancer,
on the other hand,

00:19:29.170 --> 00:19:33.920
practically picks number
of failover candidates

00:19:33.920 --> 00:19:35.310
without any configuration.

00:19:35.310 --> 00:19:36.970
All of it's done automatically.

00:19:36.970 --> 00:19:43.540
And based on the health,
size, and remaining capacity,

00:19:43.540 --> 00:19:48.465
it constantly recomputes new
failovers every two seconds.

00:19:51.690 --> 00:19:56.260
And you look at it, and
you see that lines are

00:19:56.260 --> 00:19:58.270
going a little bit far away.

00:19:58.270 --> 00:20:01.530
But what we say-- that even
though the traffic is moving

00:20:01.530 --> 00:20:04.560
in US and temporarily
you're getting

00:20:04.560 --> 00:20:07.010
a little bit higher
latency, it's

00:20:07.010 --> 00:20:11.861
much better that not
being able to serve in US.

00:20:11.861 --> 00:20:12.360
Right?

00:20:12.360 --> 00:20:18.440
Because that's what would
happen should we stay in Europe.

00:20:18.440 --> 00:20:21.650
So we're all backed
default binary.

00:20:21.650 --> 00:20:30.020
And now previously stable
binary runs without elvish.

00:20:30.020 --> 00:20:33.200
So poor elves would have
to query in English.

00:20:33.200 --> 00:20:36.350
Next year then.

00:20:36.350 --> 00:20:39.490
This is a second time when
Global Load Balancing saved us

00:20:39.490 --> 00:20:41.020
from full region outage.

00:20:43.790 --> 00:20:48.480
As the day goes by, Santa is
traveling and delivers present

00:20:48.480 --> 00:20:50.290
now in America.

00:20:50.290 --> 00:20:52.620
With a single Load
Balance [INAUDIBLE],

00:20:52.620 --> 00:20:57.490
our users experience
best latency as users

00:20:57.490 --> 00:21:02.690
from each coast come to the
close as instance groups.

00:21:02.690 --> 00:21:07.430
And as day comes to the close
and we think we're almost done,

00:21:07.430 --> 00:21:12.940
Rudolph the Reindeer went
on a famous talk show,

00:21:12.940 --> 00:21:16.660
and endorsed the Santa,
and then also said

00:21:16.660 --> 00:21:22.140
that why don't you go and check
out our favorite Santa Tracker?

00:21:22.140 --> 00:21:27.610
And sure enough, million of
peoples flock to our website,

00:21:27.610 --> 00:21:33.380
creating a perfect storm of
locally generated traffic.

00:21:33.380 --> 00:21:34.740
We are holding our breath.

00:21:34.740 --> 00:21:36.610
What is going to happen?

00:21:36.610 --> 00:21:38.330
Because there is
so much traffic,

00:21:38.330 --> 00:21:40.730
that even buffers
that Autoscaler

00:21:40.730 --> 00:21:45.970
kept for extra capacity are
being overrun immediately.

00:21:45.970 --> 00:21:50.230
But our load balancer
saves us again.

00:21:50.230 --> 00:21:52.510
Within seconds,
starting to move traffic

00:21:52.510 --> 00:21:54.640
where additional capacity is.

00:21:54.640 --> 00:22:00.340
Starting from the closest
and then going further away,

00:22:00.340 --> 00:22:02.405
if there is not
enough capacity still.

00:22:05.406 --> 00:22:11.060
In the meantime, our
Autoscaler grew back up.

00:22:11.060 --> 00:22:15.650
And the traffic caused by
Rudolph is moved back to US

00:22:15.650 --> 00:22:19.010
and is now served locally.

00:22:19.010 --> 00:22:22.960
And as viewer attention
moved on, so did the traffic.

00:22:22.960 --> 00:22:27.700
And now we are back to
our regular service.

00:22:27.700 --> 00:22:31.750
Global [INAUDIBLE] saved
us yet again-- this time,

00:22:31.750 --> 00:22:33.115
from multi-region outage.

00:22:36.890 --> 00:22:39.700
After delivering
presents in Hawaii,

00:22:39.700 --> 00:22:42.620
Santa is getting
well-deserved rest.

00:22:42.620 --> 00:22:48.360
And we can look back at our
long day and see what happened.

00:22:48.360 --> 00:22:53.660
Global Load Balancing helped
us to successfully serve

00:22:53.660 --> 00:22:57.590
huge number of [INAUDIBLE]
and averted three

00:22:57.590 --> 00:23:00.370
potential disasters.

00:23:00.370 --> 00:23:04.060
In Asia, we had an
error in configuration,

00:23:04.060 --> 00:23:07.390
which was mitigated by
cross-region overflow

00:23:07.390 --> 00:23:12.750
and then pulling back when
Autoscaler grew back up.

00:23:12.750 --> 00:23:16.790
We survived the crash of
our European instances

00:23:16.790 --> 00:23:20.670
and mitigated it by
temporarily rerouting traffic

00:23:20.670 --> 00:23:22.220
to United States.

00:23:22.220 --> 00:23:26.960
And in United
States, we rode out

00:23:26.960 --> 00:23:29.970
perfect storm of locally
generated traffic

00:23:29.970 --> 00:23:35.980
by moving traffic and dipping
in worldwide in the capacity.

00:23:35.980 --> 00:23:40.450
Obviously, I didn't
talk about all cases

00:23:40.450 --> 00:23:42.200
that could have happened.

00:23:42.200 --> 00:23:46.120
And so I encourage you to
pick your favorite case

00:23:46.120 --> 00:23:51.760
and analyze it in the presence
of Global Load Balancer.

00:23:51.760 --> 00:23:55.770
I hope this example of running
globally load-balanced, highly

00:23:55.770 --> 00:24:01.620
available service persuaded you
that global load balancing is

00:24:01.620 --> 00:24:06.750
the way to build scalable,
reliable, and highly available

00:24:06.750 --> 00:24:08.570
global load balancing.

00:24:08.570 --> 00:24:09.585
Come and try it.

00:24:09.585 --> 00:24:11.250
HTTPS, it will be on GCP.

00:24:14.130 --> 00:24:17.830
And to further learn about
best-in-class Global Load

00:24:17.830 --> 00:24:22.350
Balancer, please welcome Matt
Smart, tech lead and manager

00:24:22.350 --> 00:24:23.369
at Google.

00:24:23.369 --> 00:24:26.303
[APPLAUSE]

00:24:30.220 --> 00:24:32.380
MATT SMART: Thanks, Anna.

00:24:32.380 --> 00:24:34.900
So as Anna demonstrated,
global load balancing

00:24:34.900 --> 00:24:39.010
is an important tool to improve
reliability, and availability,

00:24:39.010 --> 00:24:41.770
and serve your users
with low latency.

00:24:41.770 --> 00:24:45.120
To even further reduce
latency for your users,

00:24:45.120 --> 00:24:49.560
you can use a Content
Delivery Network, or CDN.

00:24:49.560 --> 00:24:53.310
A CDN will cache your site's
content all around the world,

00:24:53.310 --> 00:24:57.070
speeding delivery to your users
while also offloading work

00:24:57.070 --> 00:24:58.500
from your back ends.

00:24:58.500 --> 00:25:01.600
To that end, the
Google Cloud Platform

00:25:01.600 --> 00:25:04.110
has partnered with
six CDN operators

00:25:04.110 --> 00:25:06.850
through our CDN
Interconnect Program.

00:25:06.850 --> 00:25:09.860
With CDN Interconnect, you'll
get optimized connections

00:25:09.860 --> 00:25:14.880
to your CDN of choice and
pay less for network egress.

00:25:14.880 --> 00:25:18.730
And in the coming weeks, we'll
be launching the beta release

00:25:18.730 --> 00:25:22.400
of Google Cloud CDN.

00:25:22.400 --> 00:25:25.810
Google Cloud CDN is our
own delivery network,

00:25:25.810 --> 00:25:30.340
and it integrates seamlessly
with the Global Load Balancer.

00:25:30.340 --> 00:25:32.790
We've deployed our global
load balancing stack

00:25:32.790 --> 00:25:35.140
at more than 50 points of
presence around the globe,

00:25:35.140 --> 00:25:37.100
as Prajakta mentioned earlier.

00:25:37.100 --> 00:25:38.880
And it integrates
with Google Cloud CDN

00:25:38.880 --> 00:25:42.030
at each of those locations.

00:25:42.030 --> 00:25:44.810
With Google Cloud CDN,
your cacheable content

00:25:44.810 --> 00:25:46.360
can be served
directly to your users

00:25:46.360 --> 00:25:50.510
from each of those 50 locations.

00:25:50.510 --> 00:25:52.590
Not only do you get
a world-class edge

00:25:52.590 --> 00:25:56.640
presence backed by the global
network Prajakta mentioned.

00:25:56.640 --> 00:25:59.750
Your cacheable content can
be served directly to your--

00:25:59.750 --> 00:26:03.600
you can partition your site
based on the back-end service

00:26:03.600 --> 00:26:06.400
to enable CDN for
only those portions

00:26:06.400 --> 00:26:10.550
of your back-end services
that's highly cacheable.

00:26:10.550 --> 00:26:12.990
Leave off your admin
APIs, but use it

00:26:12.990 --> 00:26:16.250
for your static content,
your CSS, your JavaScript,

00:26:16.250 --> 00:26:19.680
maybe those elvish and
English language files,

00:26:19.680 --> 00:26:23.800
all without hosting
it on a new domain.

00:26:23.800 --> 00:26:25.300
You can also analyze
how well you're

00:26:25.300 --> 00:26:27.600
doing with a cloud
monitoring, which

00:26:27.600 --> 00:26:29.830
is stacked right for
integration and cloud logging.

00:26:33.540 --> 00:26:35.577
So as we saw with
Santa, you know

00:26:35.577 --> 00:26:37.160
you're going to have
a lot of traffic,

00:26:37.160 --> 00:26:40.950
but you might not know how much.

00:26:40.950 --> 00:26:43.360
Why should you need
to send test traffic

00:26:43.360 --> 00:26:47.581
or trickles of live traffic just
to warm up your load balancers?

00:26:47.581 --> 00:26:49.580
When we created the network
load balancer, which

00:26:49.580 --> 00:26:53.190
is a precursor to the global
load balancing for TCP and UDP

00:26:53.190 --> 00:26:55.540
traffic, we wanted to
take it for a spin,

00:26:55.540 --> 00:26:58.510
so we did what any
normal Googler would do.

00:26:58.510 --> 00:27:01.220
We threw a million
queries per second at it.

00:27:01.220 --> 00:27:05.400
So Global Load Balancing, we
wanted to do the same thing.

00:27:05.400 --> 00:27:08.665
In this case, we took
it up to 1.3 million.

00:27:08.665 --> 00:27:12.150
And then we stopped, because
well, just we wanted to stop.

00:27:14.740 --> 00:27:18.020
And as you could see, this
is to a single anycast

00:27:18.020 --> 00:27:23.140
address, no DNS load balancing
required, between regions.

00:27:23.140 --> 00:27:25.550
And all this traffic is
being sunk at whatever region

00:27:25.550 --> 00:27:27.100
slow traffic is coming from.

00:27:27.100 --> 00:27:30.190
In this case, we both had
the sink and the source

00:27:30.190 --> 00:27:33.460
in these three locations listed.

00:27:33.460 --> 00:27:36.210
Again, no pre-warming necessary.

00:27:36.210 --> 00:27:37.740
Up, over, down.

00:27:41.890 --> 00:27:45.570
With Google Cloud CDN and
Global Load Balancing,

00:27:45.570 --> 00:27:48.490
we decreased latency
in a couple of ways.

00:27:48.490 --> 00:27:50.960
The first, again, is by
having these locations

00:27:50.960 --> 00:27:53.625
all around the world
physically close to your users.

00:27:56.630 --> 00:27:58.980
A second is by supporting
modern protocols,

00:27:58.980 --> 00:28:03.270
like HTTP2, which reuse the
network very well and more

00:28:03.270 --> 00:28:05.090
efficiently.

00:28:05.090 --> 00:28:08.510
So let's look at how well we do.

00:28:08.510 --> 00:28:11.610
This first section--
we've got your application

00:28:11.610 --> 00:28:13.720
deployed in a single region.

00:28:13.720 --> 00:28:16.620
In this case, US East 1.

00:28:16.620 --> 00:28:19.210
And the data is showing
median load time

00:28:19.210 --> 00:28:22.590
for 100-kilobyte files.

00:28:22.590 --> 00:28:25.670
We're doing pretty
well in New York.

00:28:25.670 --> 00:28:27.230
That's that little red bar.

00:28:27.230 --> 00:28:30.400
But the rest of the
world is pretty grumpy.

00:28:30.400 --> 00:28:35.310
And we like the
rest of the world.

00:28:35.310 --> 00:28:37.375
The second part-- we've
deployed our application

00:28:37.375 --> 00:28:38.890
in more regions.

00:28:38.890 --> 00:28:44.030
In this case, both US
regions, Asia, and Europe.

00:28:44.030 --> 00:28:48.620
It's gotten much better,
but the gold and the green,

00:28:48.620 --> 00:28:50.890
South America and
Australia, they're

00:28:50.890 --> 00:28:52.549
still not quite that happy.

00:28:52.549 --> 00:28:53.590
And we want to do better.

00:28:56.500 --> 00:28:58.660
The third part-- we've
enabled Cloud CDN.

00:29:01.330 --> 00:29:03.366
I can't see what
that says anymore.

00:29:03.366 --> 00:29:04.740
I think they're
pretty happy now.

00:29:08.610 --> 00:29:11.650
So our users depend on
Google Cloud Platform

00:29:11.650 --> 00:29:14.480
to deliver resilient
autoscaled services

00:29:14.480 --> 00:29:16.704
with a global footprint.

00:29:16.704 --> 00:29:18.620
We've described how
Global Load Balancing fits

00:29:18.620 --> 00:29:20.530
into that picture,
but you really

00:29:20.530 --> 00:29:23.000
don't have to take
my word for it.

00:29:23.000 --> 00:29:26.670
Let's hear from a power user
of Google Cloud Load Balancing.

00:29:26.670 --> 00:29:29.530
Please welcome Dmitry from Wix.

00:29:29.530 --> 00:29:31.925
[APPLAUSE]

00:29:33.809 --> 00:29:36.290
DMITRY SHESTAK: Thank you, Matt.

00:29:36.290 --> 00:29:37.020
Hi.

00:29:37.020 --> 00:29:38.160
I am Dmitry.

00:29:38.160 --> 00:29:41.490
I am solution
architect at wix.com.

00:29:41.490 --> 00:29:44.960
I want to tell you how you too
can move our services to Google

00:29:44.960 --> 00:29:45.460
Cloud.

00:29:48.160 --> 00:29:51.990
Sorry, it's going
to be this one.

00:29:51.990 --> 00:29:54.590
So what is Wix?

00:29:54.590 --> 00:29:57.020
For those of you who are not
familiar with the company,

00:29:57.020 --> 00:29:58.980
it's easy,
what-you-see-is-what-you-get

00:29:58.980 --> 00:30:00.610
service to building a website.

00:30:00.610 --> 00:30:03.040
So business owners,
even nontechnical ones,

00:30:03.040 --> 00:30:05.820
can easily build the site
for the company the way

00:30:05.820 --> 00:30:08.100
they want to see it.

00:30:08.100 --> 00:30:13.750
And the platform is built up of
many services, many products,

00:30:13.750 --> 00:30:15.890
and hundreds of services.

00:30:15.890 --> 00:30:18.390
But today I only want
to talk about one

00:30:18.390 --> 00:30:22.420
of them, which is image
serving and manipulation.

00:30:22.420 --> 00:30:24.490
So the service itself is
very simple and basic.

00:30:24.490 --> 00:30:26.550
It's exactly what
it sounds like.

00:30:26.550 --> 00:30:28.900
Site owner uploads an image.

00:30:28.900 --> 00:30:31.530
Site visitors come and
sees it on the website.

00:30:31.530 --> 00:30:33.460
But we do not serve
original images.

00:30:33.460 --> 00:30:36.050
We resize them,
adjusts them on the fly

00:30:36.050 --> 00:30:39.260
to improve user experience.

00:30:39.260 --> 00:30:40.990
So if you, like,
open the same website

00:30:40.990 --> 00:30:43.225
on a desktop and
mobile device, you

00:30:43.225 --> 00:30:46.530
will get the same images
in different dimensions.

00:30:46.530 --> 00:30:50.640
And the numbers you are seeing
here growing month after month.

00:30:50.640 --> 00:30:52.370
Just like today's snapshot.

00:30:52.370 --> 00:30:57.100
So we have mixed storage to
handle many requests to serve.

00:30:57.100 --> 00:30:59.800
And today, I have to say,
we run on active-active set

00:30:59.800 --> 00:31:02.170
upon both Google and Amazon.

00:31:02.170 --> 00:31:04.870
And I can tell you a lot
about platform differences.

00:31:04.870 --> 00:31:07.710
Subject to totally
different talk.

00:31:07.710 --> 00:31:10.840
So let's start with
some history first.

00:31:10.840 --> 00:31:15.360
Four years ago, we were running
on free, on-prem data centers.

00:31:15.360 --> 00:31:18.440
This means storing
each image three times.

00:31:18.440 --> 00:31:20.820
And the machines that were
doing the image manipulation

00:31:20.820 --> 00:31:24.870
were keeping the images
in the local storage.

00:31:24.870 --> 00:31:26.700
The downsides are quite obvious.

00:31:26.700 --> 00:31:30.020
With the growth we have, we
had to double our capacity each

00:31:30.020 --> 00:31:32.890
four or five months without
stopping the service,

00:31:32.890 --> 00:31:34.700
moving the files.

00:31:34.700 --> 00:31:35.720
Not a nice task to do.

00:31:35.720 --> 00:31:36.220
Don't do it.

00:31:36.220 --> 00:31:37.911
Please, don't.

00:31:37.911 --> 00:31:39.410
And of course,
there's the hardware.

00:31:39.410 --> 00:31:41.220
You have to keep your
maintenance going.

00:31:41.220 --> 00:31:43.280
You have controllers failing.

00:31:43.280 --> 00:31:44.660
You have [INAUDIBLE] failing.

00:31:44.660 --> 00:31:45.650
You have batteries.

00:31:45.650 --> 00:31:46.650
Just name it.

00:31:46.650 --> 00:31:48.420
Everything can go wrong.

00:31:48.420 --> 00:31:49.890
So we start moving to the cloud.

00:31:52.400 --> 00:31:54.850
And at the very beginning,
it was not easy.

00:31:54.850 --> 00:31:56.520
Many features go missing.

00:31:56.520 --> 00:31:58.330
So we had to be very creative.

00:31:58.330 --> 00:32:00.460
So what we were doing
at the very beginning.

00:32:00.460 --> 00:32:03.800
We were serving the traffic
with network load balancer

00:32:03.800 --> 00:32:05.910
through one single
region, and we

00:32:05.910 --> 00:32:08.580
were manually creating
our own instances,

00:32:08.580 --> 00:32:11.730
adjusting the size of our setup.

00:32:11.730 --> 00:32:14.670
It means if we were expecting
more traffic to hit,

00:32:14.670 --> 00:32:17.920
you were creating just enough
instances to handle it.

00:32:17.920 --> 00:32:21.665
Now, Wix strongly believes
in continuous integration

00:32:21.665 --> 00:32:23.250
and continuous delivery.

00:32:23.250 --> 00:32:26.060
With the hundreds
of services we have,

00:32:26.060 --> 00:32:30.520
have a new version hitting G8
every couple of minutes, OK?

00:32:30.520 --> 00:32:32.080
No one can predict
which one of them

00:32:32.080 --> 00:32:34.020
might affect images traffic.

00:32:34.020 --> 00:32:35.160
No one.

00:32:35.160 --> 00:32:37.692
We had to be very responsive.

00:32:37.692 --> 00:32:39.760
Not recommended.

00:32:39.760 --> 00:32:41.020
Long nights.

00:32:41.020 --> 00:32:44.970
So when Autoscaler arrived, it
just changed the way we live,

00:32:44.970 --> 00:32:48.010
changed the way we do anything.

00:32:48.010 --> 00:32:50.610
It removed this need to
configure and attune things

00:32:50.610 --> 00:32:51.840
manually.

00:32:51.840 --> 00:32:55.300
We could sleep at night finally.

00:32:55.300 --> 00:32:59.530
Autoscaler, it's a very
critical part of the system.

00:32:59.530 --> 00:33:03.580
It was mentioned before,
but put little think in it.

00:33:03.580 --> 00:33:05.580
If you have too many
instances running,

00:33:05.580 --> 00:33:07.270
you just pay with extra money.

00:33:07.270 --> 00:33:09.510
Well, if you have not
enough instances running,

00:33:09.510 --> 00:33:11.540
you have to pay with
your system latency.

00:33:11.540 --> 00:33:13.350
You pay with your
system availability.

00:33:13.350 --> 00:33:17.450
You pay with your customers, OK?

00:33:17.450 --> 00:33:20.850
And Google Autoscaler keeps
you with just right amount

00:33:20.850 --> 00:33:23.450
of instances even
at traffic peaks.

00:33:23.450 --> 00:33:25.680
And it adapts extremely fast.

00:33:29.330 --> 00:33:33.920
It took us time to put
trust in Autoscalar.

00:33:33.920 --> 00:33:36.920
Today we know we can trust
it, but it took us time.

00:33:36.920 --> 00:33:39.790
I will give you just
one example that we had.

00:33:39.790 --> 00:33:43.150
There were an autoscaled
instance group

00:33:43.150 --> 00:33:45.970
running with a metrics below
the threshold for a long time

00:33:45.970 --> 00:33:48.790
without any missed
instance being removed.

00:33:48.790 --> 00:33:49.850
Strange behavior.

00:33:49.850 --> 00:33:51.070
So we opened a support case.

00:33:51.070 --> 00:33:53.920
We had a conversation
with the team.

00:33:53.920 --> 00:33:58.350
What we realized is that
Autoscaler took care of us.

00:33:58.350 --> 00:34:01.070
Removing even one
single instance

00:34:01.070 --> 00:34:03.970
would raise the traffic,
hitting the remaining instances.

00:34:03.970 --> 00:34:06.680
That would raise the
metric above the threshold.

00:34:06.680 --> 00:34:09.530
New instances would be created.

00:34:09.530 --> 00:34:14.679
So Autoscaler was smart enough
to keep us from this flopping.

00:34:14.679 --> 00:34:16.201
It just kept us safe.

00:34:16.201 --> 00:34:17.659
Of course, also,
the health checks.

00:34:21.040 --> 00:34:24.651
When Global HTTP Load
Balancer came out,

00:34:24.651 --> 00:34:26.400
it finally allowed us
to serve the traffic

00:34:26.400 --> 00:34:28.719
from multiple regions.

00:34:28.719 --> 00:34:31.610
We could stay up whatever
happens in any single region.

00:34:31.610 --> 00:34:33.290
It was fantastic.

00:34:33.290 --> 00:34:37.800
Now we can control the traffic
to hit specific instance

00:34:37.800 --> 00:34:38.699
groups.

00:34:38.699 --> 00:34:42.670
And the opposite-- we can drain
the traffic from the others.

00:34:42.670 --> 00:34:45.320
Of course, there are additional
pieces in the puzzle.

00:34:45.320 --> 00:34:48.520
You have deployment manager.

00:34:48.520 --> 00:34:50.192
You have preemptible VMs.

00:34:50.192 --> 00:34:51.650
And by the way,
preemptible VMs are

00:34:51.650 --> 00:34:54.760
one of the reasons we
prefer the traffic to hit

00:34:54.760 --> 00:34:56.365
specific instance groups.

00:34:56.365 --> 00:34:56.989
It's fantastic.

00:34:56.989 --> 00:35:01.070
It's all easily configurable,
and it goes from zero

00:35:01.070 --> 00:35:02.595
to full scale in
matter of seconds.

00:35:05.190 --> 00:35:09.720
I will give you just this
small example of traffic.

00:35:09.720 --> 00:35:11.600
Small weekly traffic we have.

00:35:11.600 --> 00:35:14.860
You can see the traffic goes
up and down as the day goes by.

00:35:14.860 --> 00:35:18.481
And you can see the amount of
instances adjust accordingly.

00:35:18.481 --> 00:35:18.980
Magically.

00:35:18.980 --> 00:35:20.240
No hands involved.

00:35:20.240 --> 00:35:22.030
Everything's goes automatically.

00:35:22.030 --> 00:35:24.120
And you can see
the response time

00:35:24.120 --> 00:35:25.790
stays the way it should be.

00:35:25.790 --> 00:35:29.060
It stays the way we defined it.

00:35:29.060 --> 00:35:30.910
And the most exciting
things that I like,

00:35:30.910 --> 00:35:32.770
look at the bottom-right graph.

00:35:32.770 --> 00:35:34.130
You see this traffic?

00:35:34.130 --> 00:35:36.880
Spread across 14
different instance

00:35:36.880 --> 00:35:40.990
groups in different zones
of different regions.

00:35:40.990 --> 00:35:42.625
And you can easily see.

00:35:42.625 --> 00:35:45.050
When traffic of
some instance group

00:35:45.050 --> 00:35:49.470
falls down for any particular
reason-- like preemptible VMs

00:35:49.470 --> 00:35:53.140
were taken from us or we
deployed faulty version--

00:35:53.140 --> 00:35:57.000
it hands over automatically
to other instance groups,

00:35:57.000 --> 00:36:00.170
and no harms to service happens.

00:36:00.170 --> 00:36:03.000
We stay up 100%.

00:36:03.000 --> 00:36:09.610
So if you haven't tried all
this magic before, just do it.

00:36:09.610 --> 00:36:16.565
So let me hand you to Prajakta
to summarize those things.

